# A committed TEMPLATE of .github/c2-serving-job.env for the card telemetry window (scripts/ci/c2_serving_job.py parses it): copy it over .github/c2-serving-job.env on a throwaway commit of
# tp4/fusion-1 (or the integrated head) and push a tag experiment/c2-serving-vN. Nothing here names a rig, a card, a host or a registry: the cards are resolved at run time. The job is a
# template only: no tag is pushed from the branch by its author. ORDER.txt has the order, the minutes, the stop rules and how to read the telemetry.
# ONE IMAGE: C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-fusion-1 is a PLACEHOLDER for the tag the integrated pack's B0 builds from the pushed head (sed -i 's/tp4-fusion-1/<tag>/' *.env). The sidecar is not in the image:
# it runs on the host from the checkout (scripts/ci/card_telemetry.sh), so a commit that only changes it needs no new image. The cards are under development: this job never stops, starts or hands back
# the node agent.
# TEL2 (TIMED, card telemetry): the eight-live steady window (concurrent8_steady) on the production profile, with C2_TELEMETRY=1: the decode-bound counterpart of TEL1. Eight users decode at once
# for the whole window, the pack's timing shape (the same job as the integrated pack's control arms, plus the sidecar). The sidecar reads every chip's ARC telemetry once a second for the whole step
# and prints one line per chip at the end (AICLK under load against fmax, maximum power and temperature, whether any throttler engaged and which). WHY: whether a sustained decode window sits
# at fmax or is held down by a power, current or thermal limit, and whether the sidecar itself leaves the round times alone.
# READ: (1) the `[TELEMETRY]` lines of the step log (also telemetry-smoke/telemetry-summary.txt and .json in the results; the per-second rows are telemetry-smoke/telemetry.csv.gz) and `decode check ok`
# in the first one; (2) per chip: loaded AICLK median against fmax and the limiters named, as in TEL1; decode runs at a lower power than a long prefill, so a limiter here is the stronger finding;
# (3) the eight-live window PAIRED against a control job of the pack that ran without the sidecar, never by unpaired medians: python3 scripts/ci/w2ln_timing_compare.py pair <A container log> <B container log>
# --window steady --a-smoke <A smoke log> --b-smoke <B smoke log> with A = this job and B = the pack's control (FXC1 or S1C1), and its floor subcommand over two control logs: this job must sit inside the
# control-to-control floor (the sidecar is a reader and should be invisible; if it is not, say so in the report and re-run without it); the texts equal (text_mismatches empty). THE SIDECAR READS ONLY
# (AXI reads of the table the ARC firmware publishes, the read tt-smi makes; no ARC message, no write) and opens each chip for a moment per sample as the host's telemetry collector does.
# TIMED: the ARC runner sets are at ZERO for this job; no build, no CI push, no other job beside it. Duration (estimate): 35 minutes; the box is 60.
C2_CARDS=quad
C2_ACTIONS=reset smoke
C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-fusion-1
C2_PROFILE=c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-traffic
C2_SMOKE_TESTS=warmup,concurrent8_steady
C2_BOX_MINUTES=60
C2_TELEMETRY=1
